<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mu of Balhae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 719–<a href="/years/737">737</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Dae Muye, King Mu of Balhae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mu, personal name Dae Muye, was the second king of Balhae, reigning from 719 to 737, and son of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dae-joyeong">Dae Joyeong</a>. He expanded the kingdom over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> tribes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and used his own reign title. His attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heishui-mohe">Heishui Mohe</a> led to war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, including a naval raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dengzhou">Dengzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> in 732, and he also opened relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 727.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dae Joyeong's victory at Tianmenling in 698 allowed the state to form, and under his son King Mu (<b>Dae Muye</b>, r. 719–737) it expanded aggressively.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.32,127.23&amp;year=728" aria-label="Korea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7715 787 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mu of Balhae"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8165 1057h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8165 1057h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.32,127.23&amp;year=728">What was here in 728 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
